{
    "file": "Manager_Management.docx",
    "title": "Manager Management",
    "duration": "3 Days",
    "overview": [
        "Managing managers is fundamentally different from supervising individual contributors. The role requires leaders to achieve results through other leaders, create alignment across teams, strengthen managerial accountability, develop management capability, coordinate priorities, and ensure that organizational standards are translated consistently into day-to-day leadership practice.",
        "This course equips senior managers, department heads, and leaders of managers with practical methods for directing, supporting, challenging, and developing the managers who report to them. Participants examine role clarity, delegation, accountability, performance management, coaching, decision rights, cross-functional coordination, managerial communication, conflict, succession, and the creation of a strong management culture.",
        "The program emphasizes the transition from personally solving operational problems to building a capable layer of managers who can lead independently. Through leadership cases, delegation exercises, coaching scenarios, performance discussions, accountability tools, and action planning, participants will develop a structured approach to improving the performance of managers and the teams they lead."
    ],
    "audience": [
        "Senior managers, directors, department heads, business-unit leaders, functional heads, general managers, experienced managers who supervise other managers or team leaders, and high-potential leaders preparing to assume responsibility for multiple teams or managerial layers."
    ],
    "methodology": [
        "The course uses an applied leadership methodology combining short presentations, management case studies, role-clarity exercises, delegation and decision-rights activities, performance-management scenarios, coaching simulations, accountability discussions, cross-functional problem solving, difficult-conversation practice, peer feedback, and individual action planning. Participants apply the concepts to realistic challenges involved in leading managers rather than individual contributors."
    ],
    "competencies": [
        "Leadership of managers",
        "Managerial accountability",
        "Delegation and empowerment",
        "Performance management",
        "Coaching and manager development",
        "Decision-rights management",
        "Strategic alignment",
        "Cross-functional coordination",
        "Leadership communication",
        "Conflict management",
        "Succession development",
        "Management culture building"
    ],
    "objectivesIntro": [
        "By the end of the course, participants will be able to:"
    ],
    "objectives": [
        "Explain the differences between managing individual contributors and managing managers.",
        "Clarify managerial roles, responsibilities, decision rights, and performance expectations.",
        "Delegate outcomes and authority without unnecessary interference or loss of accountability.",
        "Create effective management rhythms for planning, review, communication, and follow-up.",
        "Evaluate manager performance using both business results and leadership behavior.",
        "Coach managers to strengthen judgment, leadership capability, and team performance.",
        "Address underperformance and accountability gaps constructively.",
        "Coordinate priorities and resolve conflicts across multiple teams and functions.",
        "Develop stronger managerial ownership and reduce upward dependency.",
        "Identify and develop future managers and successors.",
        "Create a practical plan for strengthening the management layer of the organization."
    ],
